WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.008
LEONARD AUSTIN: My
name's Leonard Austin.

00:00:02.008 --> 00:00:03.210
And I'm the CTO of Ravelin.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.240
Ravelin is a fraud
detection company.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.940
We specialize in detecting
credit card fraud.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.770
When a customer tries
to buy something

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.840
from one of our
merchants, the merchant

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.900
will then stream us
all of that data.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.650
And we will use a load
of machine learning

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.500
behind the scenes to try and
analyze transaction and make

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.560
predictions around whether
that user is a genuine user

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.000
or a fraudulent user.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.480
We consume a huge quantity
of data through our API.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.330
It's not just our
clients' real-time data,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.820
but it's also all of the
historic data as well.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.460
It's everything
from transactions

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.610
to customers' orders, along
with all of their transaction

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.070
history, and potentially
location data as well.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.000
That's typically terabytes
of data per client.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.100
Security is really
important for us

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.400
because our clients are trusting
us with all of their data.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.980
So we go to
extraordinary lengths

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.980
to ensure that the data
that we hold is encrypted

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.770
and securer than
closed-source projects.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.950
Cloud security, for us,
is better than on-prem.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.960
There's a lot of
aspects of on-prem

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.790
that we just can't
really facilitate.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:06.720
And Google can.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.450
So on-prem has the
problem of having

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.160
to manage your own security.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.040
If we had a warehouse of
racks and data centers,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.332
we'd have to protect ourselves.

00:01:15.332 --> 00:01:17.040
With the cloud, we
don't have to do that.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.870
So all of that is
outsourced to Google.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.594
Above and beyond that,
the actual cloud itself

00:01:21.594 --> 00:01:23.010
and the way that
Google structures

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.380
the file system
means that one file

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.835
isn't stored on one computer.

00:01:26.835 --> 00:01:29.430
It's stored across
multiple disks, which

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.680
means even if someone
was able to get access

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.930
to a particular disk,
a physical access,

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.140
they wouldn't actually be able
to get any data from that.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.230
We migrated from another
cloud provider to GCP.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.510
And the decision to move to
GCP wasn't taken lightly.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:47.220
And we did a lot of
evaluation of GCP

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.140
before deciding
to make the move.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.434
One of the major
factors that made us

00:01:51.434 --> 00:01:53.100
think that it was the
right thing to do,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.170
we could work with
our account manager

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.600
and easily get access
to the documentation

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.230
to prove to our auditors
that GCP was secure

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:02.407
and it was up to scratch.

00:02:02.407 --> 00:02:03.990
The other thing that
we are able to do

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.640
is work closely
with the KMS team.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.710
And working with those
guys, you could really

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.790
see that there was a focus
and a roadmap that really took

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.690
security to the next level.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.230
There's a number of
services that GCP offer

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.540
that we couldn't get elsewhere.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.800
An example is like the
Identity-Aware Proxy, which

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.100
you can't get anywhere else, and
encryption at rest by default,

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.700
but also the further step that
enables us to supply encryption

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.880
keys to Google, so that Google
can't even unencrypt the data

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.170
if they wanted to.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.210
The Identity-Aware
Proxy, for us,

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.210
is a really nice service,
because it basically

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:39.180
enables us to protect all
of our internal dashboards

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.730
without having the
complicated setup of a VPN

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:46.110
and having to get our users
to install VPN clients,

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.450
install credentials,
have two-factor.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.970
It's all baked into the
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.430
And because we're used
as a Google app engine,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.860
it just means that there's
no additional set-up

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.640
for anyone within the company.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.400
So those are all
really strong factors

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.040
that suggest that Google
really cares about security.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.380
And it enables us to trust
Google with all of our data.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

